Chapter 1: Introduction and Vision

The dominant paradigm of artificial intelligence treats cognition as a sequence of discrete computations: an input is mapped to an output via a model, after which the system resets and waits for the next query. While this has produced remarkable results, it is fundamentally at odds with the continuous, persistent, and distributed nature of biological intelligence. In living systems, neural tissue never resets; memory is non‑Markovian, knowledge propagates as wave‑like patterns across cortical sheets, and every sensory stream continuously refines an internal generative model of the world.

Cognitive Field Computing (CFC) is proposed as a unified alternative. Its core postulate is that intelligence can be treated as a field—a quantity \Psi(\mathbf{x},t) that exists at every point \mathbf{x} in a physical or information space and at every instant of time t. This field is continuously updated by three mechanisms: diffusion of knowledge (spreading of information across the field), local generative inference (active inference driven by sensor streams S and memory M), and global alignment through the collective behavior of the agents that sample and contribute to the field. The field never stops; its state at time t+\Delta t is an update of its state at time t, not a reboot.

The initial conceptual equation,

\frac{\partial \Psi}{\partial t} = \alpha \nabla^2 \Psi + F(\Psi, S, M),

served as a visionary placeholder. This thesis provides the rigorous mathematics that makes every term precise, computable, and physically realizable. We replace the ordinary time derivative by a fractional derivative of order \alpha \in (0,1] to encode long‑term memory without a separate memory store. The diffusion term is equipped with a state‑dependent coefficient D(\dot{S}_{\text{field}}) that enforces thermodynamic bounds, ensuring the field remains stable on real hardware. The reaction term F is derived from the variational free energy principle of active inference, augmented by a bounded‑uncertainty operator that balances information gain against local resource limitations expressed through a resource tensor \mathbf{C}(\mathbf{x},t) and an uncertainty manifold \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x},t). The collective behavior of a swarm of agents is captured by a mean‑field game, whose forward‑backward PDE system emerges naturally from microscopic active inference. Information geometry provides a Riemannian metric on the cognitive state space, allowing us to measure distances between beliefs and to regularize learning via symmetry principles. Finally, category theory guarantees that when cognitive modules are composed—for instance, when adding a new robot sub‑swarm—the overall system’s free energy does not explode, preserving the second‑law‑like stability we call Stable Recursive Intelligence (SRI).

This dissertation constructs the full mathematical edifice. Chapter 2 assembles the necessary preliminaries: fractional calculus, functional analysis, information geometry, active inference, mean‑field games, and categorical compositionality. Chapter 3 develops the core fractional reaction–diffusion equation, proves its well‑posedness, and endows it with thermodynamic constraints. Chapter 4 derives the generative engine from active inference, formulates the bounded‑uncertainty operator, and couples the field with information‑geometric flows. Subsequent chapters (5–7, to be completed in the full manuscript) will cover the mean‑field game derivation, categorical compositionality, symmetry regularization, and the unified existence and stability theorem. The appendices provide full proofs for the mean‑field game limit and the categorical free‑energy bound.

The goal is nothing less than a mathematical blueprint for an intelligence infrastructure as ubiquitous and reliable as the electrical grid: a cognitive field that can span planetary robot swarms, AI‑native operating systems, and self‑evolving scientific research platforms, all while respecting the hard constraints of physics and computation.

