Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

This chapter recalls the essential mathematical machinery. We assume familiarity with measure theory, functional analysis, and probability, but we provide self‑contained definitions for the more specialized tools.

2.1 Function Spaces and Operators

Let \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d be a bounded domain with smooth boundary. We work with the Hilbert space H = L^2(\Omega). The L^2 inner product is \langle f,g \rangle = \int_\Omega f g\,dx, and the norm \|f\|. The Sobolev space H^1(\Omega) is the set of L^2 functions whose weak derivatives are also in L^2, with norm \|f\|_{H^1}^2 = \|f\|^2 + \|\nabla f\|^2. The space H^{-1}(\Omega) is its dual.

A linear operator A: D(A) \subset H \to H is sectorial if its resolvent set contains a sector of the complex plane and the resolvent satisfies \|(\lambda I - A)^{-1}\| \le M/|\lambda| on that sector. Sectorial operators generate analytic semigroups.

The Neumann Laplacian \Delta with domain D(\Delta) = \{u\in H^2(\Omega): \partial_\mathbf{n} u|_{\partial\Omega}=0\} is a negative self‑adjoint operator; -\Delta is sectorial and generates an analytic semigroup of contractions.

2.2 Fractional Calculus

For 0<\alpha<1, the Caputo fractional derivative of a function f\in C^1([0,T]; H) is defined by

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha f(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{f'(s)}{(t-s)^\alpha}\,ds.

The Riemann–Liouville fractional integral is  {}_0 I_t^\alpha f(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)}\int_0^t (t-s)^{\alpha-1} f(s)\,ds. A key property is  {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \circ {}_0 I_t^\alpha f = f for absolutely continuous f.

The Mittag‑Leffler functions E_\alpha(z) and E_{\alpha,\beta}(z) are the entire functions

E_\alpha(z) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{z^k}{\Gamma(\alpha k + 1)}, \qquad
E_{\alpha,\beta}(z) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty \frac{z^k}{\Gamma(\alpha k + \beta)}.

They generalize the exponential: E_1(z)=e^z. For a sectorial operator A, the fractional Cauchy problem

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha u(t) = A u(t), \quad u(0)=u_0,

has a unique mild solution given by u(t) = E_\alpha(t^\alpha A) u_0. The solution family \{E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\}_{t\ge 0} is an analytic and strongly continuous semigroup (actually a solution operator of Mittag‑Leffler type) with the estimate \|E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\| \le M e^{\omega t} for some constants.

2.3 Riemannian Geometry and Information Geometry

A statistical manifold is a set \mathcal{M} of probability densities p(x|\theta) parameterized by \theta = (\theta^1,\dots,\theta^n) \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^n. The Fisher information metric is the Riemannian metric

g_{ij}(\theta) = \int p(x|\theta)\,\frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta^i}\frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \theta^j}\,dx.

This metric is invariant under sufficient statistics and defines a natural notion of distance on the space of beliefs. The Fisher–Rao distance between two densities is the geodesic distance under g. The gradient of a function \mathcal{F} with respect to g is \mathrm{grad}_g \mathcal{F} = g^{ij} \partial_j \mathcal{F}, where g^{ij} is the inverse matrix.

2.4 Variational Free Energy and Active Inference

Consider an agent with a generative model p(o,s) over observations o and hidden states s. The agent maintains an approximate posterior q(s). The variational free energy is

\mathcal{F}[q] = \int q(s) \ln \frac{q(s)}{p(o,s)}\,ds = D_{\mathrm{KL}}[q(s) \| p(s|o)] - \ln p(o).

Minimizing \mathcal{F} with respect to q simultaneously makes q a good approximation of the true posterior and maximizes model evidence. In active inference, actions a are chosen to minimize expected free energy, which balances epistemic value (information gain) and instrumental value (goal seeking).

2.5 Mean‑Field Games

A mean‑field game describes the interaction of a large number N of identical agents. In the limit N\to\infty, the population is represented by a density m(x,t) evolving forward in time via a Fokker–Planck equation, while each agent’s optimal control leads to a value function u(x,t) evolving backward via a Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJB) equation:

\begin{aligned}
-\partial_t u - \nu \Delta u + H(x,\nabla u) &= f[m], \\
\partial_t m - \nu \Delta m - \nabla\cdot(m \nabla_p H(x,\nabla u)) &= 0.
\end{aligned}

Here H is the Hamiltonian obtained from the running cost. Under convexity and Lipschitz assumptions, existence and uniqueness can be proved via fixed‑point arguments.

2.6 Category Theory and Monads

A monad on a category \mathbf{C} is a triple (T,\eta,\mu) where T:\mathbf{C}\to\mathbf{C} is an endofunctor, \eta: \mathrm{Id}\Rightarrow T (unit), and \mu: T^2 \Rightarrow T (multiplication) satisfying coherence laws. The Giry monad on the category of measurable spaces assigns to each space X the space of probability measures P(X). Kleisli composition of two maps f: X\to T Y and g: Y\to T Z is given by (g\circ_K f)(x) = \int_Y g(y) \, df_x(y).

2.7 Thermodynamics of Computation

The entropy production rate of a physical system is \dot{S} = \int \sigma\,dx, where the local entropy production \sigma satisfies \sigma \ge 0 (second law). For a field obeying a reaction–diffusion equation, one can derive \sigma from the thermodynamic fluxes and forces. A simplified form used in this thesis is \dot{S}_{\text{field}} = \int_\Omega \frac{\|\nabla \Psi\|^2}{T} + \frac{1}{T}F\Psi\,dx. Hardware limitations impose an upper bound \dot{S}_{\text{field}} \le \gamma \mathcal{T}_{\max} - \eta \|\nabla\Psi\|^2, coupling computation to cooling capacity.

