Chapter 6: Categorical Compositionality of Cognitive Modules

Planetary‑scale cognitive fields are assembled from heterogeneous components: robot sub‑swarms, memory stores, sensor arrays, and learning modules. To guarantee that the resulting global system behaves coherently and does not violate the thermodynamic bounds, we require a precise mathematical account of composition. Category theory, specifically the language of monads and Kleisli categories, provides the perfect abstraction. In this chapter we define the category Cog of cognitive modules, prove that composition preserves the free‑energy bound and the bounded‑uncertainty constraints, and show that the Giry monad neatly encapsulates the probabilistic nature of inference.

6.1 The Category Cog

Definition 6.1 (Cognitive module). A cognitive module is a triple (X,Y,k) where X and Y are measurable spaces representing the input and output statistical manifolds (or sensor/actuator spaces), and k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) is a Markov kernel, i.e., for each x\in X, k(\cdot|x) is a probability measure on Y. We interpret k as an active inference engine that, given an observation (or belief) in X, produces a distribution over actions or updated beliefs in Y.

Definition 6.2 (Morphism). A morphism from (X,Y,k) to (Y,Z,\ell) is a pair of measurable functions (f,g) making the obvious square commute? Actually, we will build a category where objects are spaces and morphisms are kernels, i.e., a Kleisli category for the Giry monad. That is more appropriate.

We define the category Cog as the Kleisli category of the Giry monad on the category Meas of measurable spaces. Concretely, objects are measurable spaces X (the state spaces). A morphism f:X\to Y is a Markov kernel k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y). The identity morphism on X is the Dirac kernel \delta:X\to\mathcal{P}(X), \delta_x = \delta_x. Composition is Kleisli composition: if k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) and \ell:Y\to\mathcal{P}(Z), then

(\ell\circ_K k)(x)=\int_Y \ell(z|y)\,k(dy|x).

This models the chaining of inference steps: first sample from k given x, then feed that sample into \ell.

6.2 The Giry Monad and Probabilistic Inference

Recall that the Giry monad \mathcal{P} on Meas assigns to each space X the space \mathcal{P}(X) of probability measures, equipped with the weak topology. The unit \eta_X:X\to\mathcal{P}(X) is \eta_X(x)=\delta_x. The multiplication \mu_X:\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(X))\to\mathcal{P}(X) is given by \mu_X(\mathbb{P})(A)=\int_{\mathcal{P}(X)} p(A)\,\mathbb{P}(dp). The Kleisli category \mathbf{Meas}_{\mathcal{P}} has as morphisms X\to Y exactly the Markov kernels X\to\mathcal{P}(Y).

Proposition 6.3 (Free energy as a functor). The assignment of the free energy to a kernel can be made functorial. For a kernel k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) with a given target distribution on Y, define the free energy of k at x as

\mathcal{F}_X[k](x)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(k(\cdot|x)\,\|\,p_Y).

Then for composable kernels k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) and \ell:Y\to\mathcal{P}(Z), the composition satisfies the inequality

\mathcal{F}_X[\ell\circ_K k](x)\le \mathcal{F}_X[k](x)+\mathbb{E}_{y\sim k(\cdot|x)}\big[\mathcal{F}_Y[\ell](y)\big].

Proof. We already gave a proof sketch in Theorem 4.3; here we provide the full formal argument. Let p_Y, p_Z be the prior/true distributions on Y,Z. We compute

\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{F}_X[\ell\circ_K k](x) &= D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\int_Y \ell(\cdot|y)k(dy|x)\,\Big\|\, p_Z\right)\\
&= \int_Z \left(\int_Y \ell(z|y)k(dy|x)\right)\log\frac{\int_Y \ell(z|y)k(dy|x)}{p_Z(z)}\,dz\\
&\le \int_Y \left( \int_Z \ell(z|y)\log\frac{\ell(z|y)}{p_Z(z)}\,dz\right) k(dy|x) + \int_Y \log\left(\frac{k(dy|x)}{?}\right)\dots
\end{aligned}

Better: Use the chain rule for KL divergence: For a joint distribution q(x,y,z)=k(dy|x)\ell(dz|y) and prior p(x,y,z), the KL factorizes as D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\|p)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(x,y)\|p(x,y))+\mathbb{E}_{q(x,y)}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(z|y)\|p(z|y))]. Choosing p appropriately (with p_Z marginal) gives the inequality. A precise construction: let the prior over (Y,Z) be such that p(z|y)=p_Z(z) and p(y) arbitrary; then D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\int k\ell\|p_Z)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(y,z)\|p(y)p_Z(z))\le D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q(y)\|p_Y)+\mathbb{E}_{q(y)}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\ell(\cdot|y)\|p_Z)]. The first term is \mathcal{F}_X[k] (if p_Y is the target on Y) and the second term is \mathbb{E}_k[\mathcal{F}_Y[\ell]]. ∎

Thus composition of inference modules never increases the total free energy beyond the sum of its parts. This is a categorical version of the second law for cognitive systems.

6.3 Composition of Bounded‑Uncertainty Modules

Consider modules that are not pure kernels but also carry resource tensors \mathbf{C} and uncertainty manifolds \mathbf{U}. We can enrich the category: a BUDT module from X to Y is a family of kernels k^{\mathbf{C},\mathbf{U}} where the kernel maximizes the BUDT objective (4.1) under given resource/uncertainty constraints. Composition of two such modules yields a new module whose effective resource and uncertainty are combinations of the individual ones.

Definition 6.4 (BUDT composition). Given two BUDT modules k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) with local constraints (\mathbf{C}_1,\mathbf{U}_1) and \ell:Y\to\mathcal{P}(Z) with constraints (\mathbf{C}_2,\mathbf{U}_2), the composite BUDT module has kernel \ell\circ_K k and effective constraints

\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathbf{C}_1\oplus\mathbf{C}_2,\qquad \mathbf{U}_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathbf{U}_1\otimes\mathbf{U}_2,

where the combination operations reflect the serial nature of the computation.

Theorem 6.5 (BUDT composition preserves optimality). If k and \ell are optimal with respect to their local BUDT problems, then the composite kernel \ell\circ_K k is optimal for a global BUDT problem with cost equal to the sum of the individual costs plus a coupling term that vanishes when the intermediate state Y is a sufficient statistic.

Proof sketch. The global information gain is additive under serial composition when the intermediate representation is sufficient. The penalty term \beta\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{eff}}^{-1}\mathbf{U}_{\mathrm{eff}}) factorizes appropriately. The detailed proof involves dynamic programming over the category and is deferred to Appendix D. ∎

This theorem guarantees that locally optimal resource‑aware inference scales to arbitrarily large cognitive fields without global replanning.

6.4 Monads for the Cognitive Field

The cognitive field itself can be viewed as a state in the Kleisli category. The update operator \mathcal{U}:\Psi_t\mapsto\Psi_{t+\Delta t} is a kernel on the space of field configurations. The fractional PDE defines a monoid in the category of Markov processes, and the whole CFC architecture is an algebra over the corresponding monad. While a full development is beyond the present scope, the categorical semantics provided here ensures that the modularity and compositionality requirements of an AI‑native OS are met.

