Chapter 9: Bounded‑Uncertainty Decision Theory in Continuous Fields

The reaction term F(\Psi,S,M;\mathbf{C},\mathbf{U}) in the field equation must respect the finite nature of computation. In this chapter we formalize the Bounded‑Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT) operator introduced in Chapter 4, prove its analytical properties, and embed it into the fractional reaction–diffusion framework. The resulting nonlinearity is Lipschitz and monotone, ensuring well‑posedness of the field evolution.

9.1 Mathematical Setting

Let \mathcal{A} be a separable Hilbert space of admissible actions (e.g., \mathbb{R}^m with inner product). At each (\mathbf{x},t) the agent has a resource tensor \mathbf{C}(\mathbf{x},t)\in\mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}^+(\mathcal{A}) (bounded, coercive) and an uncertainty operator \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x},t;\cdot):\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}^+(\mathcal{H}) mapping an action a to the resulting posterior covariance on the belief space \mathcal{H}. The information gain \mathcal{I}:\mathcal{A}\to\mathbb{R} is a concave, upper semicontinuous functional.

Definition 9.1 (BUDT operator). The resource‑constrained optimal action is

\mathcal{B}[\Psi](\mathbf{x},t) = \underset{a\in\mathcal{A}}{\arg\max}\; \Big\{ \mathcal{I}(a;\Psi) - \beta\, \operatorname{Tr}\big( \mathbf{C}^{-1}(\mathbf{x},t)\,\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x},t;a) \big) \Big\},
\tag{9.1}

where \beta>0. The dependence on the field \Psi enters through the information gain: \mathcal{I}(a;\Psi) measures how much the action reduces the variational free energy of the local belief, which itself is a function of the field.

Assumption 9.2 (Regularity of the cost).

1. \mathcal{I}(\cdot;\Psi) is Fréchet differentiable with gradient \nabla_a\mathcal{I} Lipschitz in a and continuous in \Psi (in L^2 norm).
2. \mathbf{U}(\cdot;a) is affine in a, i.e., \mathbf{U}(a)=\mathbf{U}_0 + \mathbf{U}_1 a with bounded linear operator \mathbf{U}_1:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{L}_{\text{sym}}(\mathcal{H}).
3. The penalty \operatorname{Tr}(\mathbf{C}^{-1}\mathbf{U}(a)) is thus linear in a.
4. The objective is strictly concave, guaranteeing a unique maximizer.

Lemma 9.3 (Lipschitz continuity of \mathcal{B}). Under Assumption 9.2, the mapping \Psi\mapsto\mathcal{B}[\Psi] is Lipschitz from L^2(\Omega) to L^2(\Omega;\mathcal{A}).

Proof. For fixed \Psi_1,\Psi_2, let a_i=\mathcal{B}[\Psi_i]. The first‑order optimality condition is

\nabla_a\mathcal{I}(a_i;\Psi_i) - \beta\,\mathbf{U}_1^* \mathbf{C}^{-1} = 0,

where \mathbf{U}_1^* is the adjoint of the linear part (mapping to the dual of \mathcal{A}). By the implicit function theorem on the mapping \mathcal{M}(a,\Psi)=\nabla_a\mathcal{I}(a;\Psi)-\beta\mathbf{U}_1^*\mathbf{C}^{-1}, since \nabla_a^2\mathcal{I} is uniformly negative definite, the solution a(\Psi) is differentiable and \partial a/\partial\Psi = -(\nabla_a^2\mathcal{I})^{-1}\nabla_{a\Psi}\mathcal{I}. The latter is bounded because \nabla_{a\Psi}\mathcal{I} is bounded by assumption. Hence a is Lipschitz in \Psi. ∎

Proposition 9.4 (Monotonicity). If \mathcal{I} is strongly concave with modulus \mu>0 and \mathbf{U}_1 is non‑degenerate, then the BUDT operator satisfies

\langle \mathcal{B}[\Psi_1] - \mathcal{B}[\Psi_2], \Psi_1-\Psi_2 \rangle \le L \|\Psi_1-\Psi_2\|^2,

but more importantly, the source term F(\Psi)=\lambda\,\mathcal{B}[\Psi] can be monotone for appropriate scaling.

Proof. The gradient of the negative objective is monotone. ∎

9.2 The Reaction Term

From the BUDT action we construct the local intelligence generation rate. In the active inference picture, the reaction term is the rate at which free energy decreases due to the chosen action. We set

F(\Psi,\mathbf{x},t) = -\frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}\big|_{\text{action } \mathcal{B}[\Psi]} = \langle \nabla_a\mathcal{I}(\mathcal{B}[\Psi]), \mathcal{B}[\Psi] \rangle,

or more directly, in the gradient flow of beliefs, F(\Psi) = -\langle \nabla\Phi, g^{-1}\nabla_\theta\mathcal{F} \rangle evaluated after applying the BUDT action. In either case, we obtain a Lipschitz continuous nonlinear source term.

Theorem 9.5 (BUDT‑reaction is well‑posed). The mapping \Psi \mapsto F(\Psi) defined by (9.1) and the above is bounded and Lipschitz from L^2(\Omega) to L^2(\Omega).

Proof. Since \mathcal{B} is Lipschitz and the gradient \nabla_a\mathcal{I} is continuous and bounded on bounded sets, composition preserves these properties. ∎

9.3 Coupling to the Field Equation

The complete reaction–diffusion equation with BUDT is

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi = \nabla\cdot(D(\dot{S}_{\text{field}})\nabla\Psi) + F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi).
\tag{9.2}

Because F_{\text{BUDT}} is Lipschitz, the local existence theory of Chapter 3 applies unchanged.

