Chapter 11: Fractional Calculus and Non‑Markovian Memory in Cognitive Fields

The replacement of the ordinary time derivative with a Caputo fractional derivative of order \alpha\in(0,1] endows the field with a long‑term memory. In this chapter, we present a complete mathematical theory of the fractional Cauchy problem for the reaction–diffusion equation, prove global existence via Mittag‑Leffler operators, and connect the memory length to the spectral dimension of the cognitive manifold.

11.1 The Fractional Cauchy Problem

Let A be a closed linear operator on a Banach space X that generates a strongly continuous semigroup (or, for our purposes, an analytic semigroup). The abstract problem is

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha u(t) = A u(t) + f(t), \quad u(0)=u_0.
\tag{11.1}

Definition 11.1 (Mild solution). A function u\in C([0,T];X) is a mild solution if

u(t) = E_\alpha(t^\alpha A) u_0 + \int_0^t (t-s)^{\alpha-1} E_{\alpha,\alpha}((t-s)^\alpha A) f(s)\,ds,
\tag{11.2}

where the Mittag‑Leffler operators are defined via the Dunford integral

E_\alpha(t^\alpha A) = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{\Gamma} E_\alpha(t^\alpha z) (zI-A)^{-1}dz,

and similarly for E_{\alpha,\alpha}.

Theorem 11.2 (Well‑posedness for sectorial A). Assume A is sectorial with angle <\pi(1-\alpha/2). Then for every u_0\in X and f\in L^1(0,T;X) Hölder continuous, there exists a unique mild solution given by (11.2). Moreover,

\|E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\| \le M e^{\omega t}, \qquad \|(t-s)^{\alpha-1}E_{\alpha,\alpha}((t-s)^\alpha A)\| \le M (t-s)^{\alpha-1}.

Proof. See Prüss (1993), Evolutionary Integral Equations and Applications. The sectorial condition ensures the resolvent bounds needed for the Dunford integral. ∎

For our application, A = \nabla\cdot(D\nabla\cdot) with Neumann boundary conditions is sectorial on L^2(\Omega), satisfying the angle condition for any \alpha\in(0,1].

11.2 Nonlinear Problem with Lipschitz f

Consider f(t)=F(\Psi(t)) with F globally Lipschitz. The mild formulation becomes

\Psi(t) = E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)\Psi_0 + \int_0^t (t-s)^{\alpha-1} E_{\alpha,\alpha}((t-s)^\alpha A) F(\Psi(s))\,ds.
\tag{11.3}

Theorem 11.3 (Local and global existence). Let F be Lipschitz. Then there exists a unique local mild solution \Psi\in C([0,T];X). If F is additionally bounded, the solution exists globally.

Proof. Use the contraction mapping principle on the space C([0,\tau];X) with the weighted norm \sup_t e^{-L t}\|\Psi(t)\|. The Volterra kernel (t-s)^{\alpha-1} is integrable, yielding a contraction for small \tau. Global existence follows because the Mittag‑Leffler operators are bounded and the nonlinearity is bounded, preventing finite‑time blow‑up. ∎

11.3 Memory Kernel and Fading Memory

The integral in (11.2) shows that the solution depends on the entire history of f(s) from 0 to t. The influence of the past is weighted by (t-s)^{\alpha-1}, which decays polynomially. The characteristic memory time \tau_{\text{mem}} can be defined as the time after which the kernel drops below a threshold \epsilon:

\tau_{\text{mem}} = \left(\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{\epsilon}\right)^{1/(1-\alpha)}.

As \alpha\to1, \tau_{\text{mem}}\to0, recovering the memoryless case. As \alpha\to0, memory becomes infinitely long (the system becomes an integrator).

11.4 Relation to the Geometry of the Cognitive Manifold

The fractional order \alpha is not a free parameter but dictated by the spectral dimension d_s of the cognitive manifold (Chapter 5). The Laplace–Beltrami operator \Delta_g on the Fisher metric has eigenvalue asymptotics N(\lambda)\sim \lambda^{d_s/2}. A fractional diffusion process on this manifold with order \alpha has mean squared displacement scaling as t^{\alpha/d_s}. Matching the memory persistence of the field to the intrinsic geometry yields

\alpha = \frac{2}{d_s}.
\tag{11.4}

Thus, if the belief space has fractal or high‑dimensional structure, \alpha is small and the field exhibits strong memory. This provides a first‑principles derivation of the fractional order.

Proposition 11.4 (Spectral dimension and memory). If the Fisher metric induces a spectral dimension d_s, then the optimal fractional order for the field that respects the informational geometry is \alpha = 2/d_s.

Proof sketch. The return probability of a fractional diffusion on a Riemannian manifold with spectral dimension d_s is P(t)\sim t^{-d_s/(2\alpha)}. The decay of the memory kernel in the field should match this natural information decay, giving d_s/(2\alpha)=1\Rightarrow\alpha=2/d_s. ∎

