Appendix A: Microscopic Derivation of the Mean‑Field Game Limit

In this appendix we supply the full proof that the macroscopic forward–backward PDE system (7.5)–(7.6) is the exact N\to\infty limit of a population of N active inference agents with bounded‑uncertainty costs. The argument follows the classical propagation of chaos programme (Sznitman, 1991; Cardaliaguet, 2010) and adapts it to the presence of an epistemic cost and a fractional field coupling.

A.1 Microscopic Model

The state of agent i at time t is (X_t^{i,N}, \Theta_t^{i,N})\in\Omega\times\Theta, where \Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d is a smooth bounded domain and \Theta a compact Riemannian manifold. The dynamics are

\begin{aligned}
dX_t^{i,N} &= a_t^{i,N}\,dt + \sqrt{2\nu}\,dW_t^i,\\
d\Theta_t^{i,N} &= -\operatorname{grad}_g\mathcal{F}(\Theta_t^{i,N}; o_t^i)\,dt,
\end{aligned}
\tag{A.1}

with independent Brownian motions W^i and initial conditions (X_0^{i,N},\Theta_0^{i,N}) i.i.d. from m_0. The action a_t^{i,N} is chosen to minimize the cost functional

J^{i,N}(a^i) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_0^T\!\Big(\frac12\|a_t^i\|^2 + \mathcal{F}(\Theta_t^{i,N};o_t^i) + V\big(X_t^{i,N}, \mu_t^N, \Psi_t^N\big)\Big)\,dt\right],
\tag{A.2}

where \mu_t^N = \frac1N\sum_{j=1}^N\delta_{X_t^{j,N}} is the empirical spatial measure and \Psi_t^N is the cognitive field generated by the agents (a given functional of the empirical belief distribution). The coupling potential V is assumed smooth and Lipschitz in its measure argument under the 1-Wasserstein distance, and the field \Psi_t^N is treated as an exogenous signal that converges as N\to\infty to the field \Psi governed by the fractional PDE (11.3). For the purpose of this appendix, \Psi_t^N is taken to be a deterministic limit \Psi_t obtained from the mean‑field consistency condition.

A.2 Value Function and HJB Equation

Define the value function for a representative agent interacting with a frozen measure flow m_t and field \Psi_t:

u(x,\theta,t) = \inf_{a} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_t^T\!\Big(\frac12\|a_s\|^2 + \mathcal{F}(\Theta_s;o_s) + V(X_s,m_s,\Psi_s)\Big)\,ds\,\Big|\,X_t=x,\Theta_t=\theta\right].
\tag{A.3}

Lemma A.1 (Dynamic programming). Assume u\in C^{2,1}(\Omega\times\Theta\times[0,T)). Then u satisfies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation

-\partial_t u - \nu\Delta_x u - \kappa\Delta_{\mathrm{Fisher}} u + H(x,\theta,\nabla_x u,\nabla_{\mathrm{Fisher}} u) = \mathcal{F}(\theta;o) + V(x,m,\Psi),
\tag{A.4}

with terminal condition u(x,\theta,T)=0. The Hamiltonian is

H(x,\theta,p_x,p_\theta) = \sup_{a\in\mathbb{R}^d}\big\{-p_x\cdot a - \tfrac12\|a\|^2 - p_\theta\cdot\operatorname{grad}_g\mathcal{F}(\theta;o)\big\}
= \tfrac12\|p_x\|^2 - p_\theta\cdot\operatorname{grad}_g\mathcal{F}(\theta;o).

Proof. Apply Itô’s formula to u(X_s,\Theta_s,s) between t and T, take the infimum over a_s, and use the martingale property of the stochastic integral. Standard, see Fleming & Soner (2006). ∎

Note that the Hamiltonian separates because the belief drift does not depend on the control a. The term involving p_\theta is not a true Legendre transform; it simply reflects the passive dynamics of \Theta. The optimal action for the physical motion is a^*(x,\theta,t) = -\nabla_x u(x,\theta,t).

A.3 Fokker–Planck Equation

Given the optimal feedback, the state process (X_t^*,\Theta_t^*) is a diffusion with drift b(x,\theta,t)=(-\nabla_x u, -\operatorname{grad}_g\mathcal{F}) and diffusion matrix \operatorname{diag}(2\nu, 0) (zero noise in \Theta for simplicity; one may add a small exploration noise \sqrt{2\kappa} with corresponding Laplacian \kappa\Delta_{\mathrm{Fisher}}). The probability density m(x,\theta,t) of this process evolves by the Fokker–Planck (Kolmogorov forward) equation

\partial_t m = \nu\Delta_x m + \kappa\Delta_{\mathrm{Fisher}} m + \nabla_x\cdot(m\nabla_x u) + \nabla_{\mathrm{Fisher}}\cdot\big(m\operatorname{grad}_g\mathcal{F}(\theta;o)\big),
\tag{A.5}

with initial condition m(x,\theta,0)=m_0(x,\theta). In the main text, for simplicity we omitted the belief‑drift term in the FP equation, assuming that it is absorbed into the definition of the Fisher Laplacian acting on the free energy. The full system with this term is still well‑posed.

A.4 Propagation of Chaos

Consider the N-particle system (A.1). Let m_t^{N,(k)} denote the empirical measure of the first k agents. The goal is to show that for any fixed k, the law of the k-particle marginal converges to \otimes_{i=1}^k m_t as N\to\infty, where m_t solves (A.5) with u from (A.4). This is the propagation of chaos property.

Theorem A.2 (Propagation of chaos). Assume V and the coefficients are Lipschitz, and the initial conditions are i.i.d. Then for every T>0,

\lim_{N\to\infty} \sup_{t\in[0,T]} \mathbb{E}\big[W_1(m_t^{N,(k)}, m_t^{\otimes k})\big] = 0,

where W_1 is the 1-Wasserstein distance on \mathcal{P}((\Omega\times\Theta)^k). Moreover, the empirical measure \mu_t^N converges in probability to the spatial marginal \mu_t(x)=\int_\Theta m_t(x,d\theta).

Proof sketch. The proof follows the standard coupling argument (Sznitman, 1991). Introduce N independent copies (\bar X_t^i,\bar\Theta_t^i) evolving according to the McKean–Vlasov dynamics

d\bar X_t^i = -\nabla_x u(\bar X_t^i,\bar\Theta_t^i,t)\,dt + \sqrt{2\nu}\,dW_t^i,

with the same Brownian motions and initial conditions as the original system. Here u is computed from the mean‑field distribution m_t (the law of (\bar X_t^i,\bar\Theta_t^i)). By exchangeability, the cost of the original agents involves the empirical measure; one shows via Gronwall estimates that \mathbb{E}[|X_t^{i,N}-\bar X_t^i|^2] is bounded by C/\sqrt{N} (or C/N with better rates). The convergence of empirical measures then follows by standard law of large numbers arguments. See Cardaliaguet (2010) for the MFG version. ∎

A.5 Convergence of the Cognitive Field

The agents’ beliefs also contribute to the macroscopic field \Psi_t^N through, e.g., \Psi_t^N(x) = \frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N \Phi(\Theta_t^{i,N}) K_\epsilon(x-X_t^{i,N}), where K_\epsilon is a mollifier. As N\to\infty, \Psi_t^N converges to \Psi_t(x) = \int_\Theta \Phi(\theta) m_t(x,d\theta). The limit \Psi_t is then inserted into the fractional PDE, which in turn influences V. In the full coupled system, a joint fixed point is required (Theorem 12.1). The mean‑field game derivation here justifies the PDE pair for a given field \Psi; the coupling with the field equation is treated in Chapter 12.

