Appendix B: The Giry Monad and Categorical Compositionality

We give the complete categorical construction of the Giry monad on the category of measurable spaces, define the Kleisli category of cognitive modules, and prove the free‑energy bound under Kleisli composition (Theorem 6.3/4.3). We adopt the measure‑theoretic framework of Giry (1982) and Panangaden (2009).

B.1 The Category Meas

Let Meas be the category whose objects are measurable spaces (X,\Sigma_X) and whose morphisms are measurable functions. For any object X, the set \mathcal{P}(X) of probability measures on X becomes a measurable space when equipped with the \sigma-algebra generated by the evaluation maps ev_A: \mathcal{P}(X)\to[0,1], \mu\mapsto\mu(A), for all A\in\Sigma_X.

Definition B.1 (Giry functor). \mathcal{P}: \mathbf{Meas}\to\mathbf{Meas} is defined on objects as \mathcal{P}(X) and on morphisms f:X\to Y by \mathcal{P}(f)(\mu) = \mu\circ f^{-1} (the pushforward). The unit \eta_X: X\to\mathcal{P}(X) is \eta_X(x)=\delta_x (Dirac measure). The multiplication \mu_X: \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(X))\to\mathcal{P}(X) is given by \mu_X(\mathbb{P})(A) = \int_{\mathcal{P}(X)} p(A)\,\mathbb{P}(dp). One verifies that (\mathcal{P},\eta,\mu) satisfies the monad laws.

B.2 The Kleisli Category \mathbf{Meas}_{\mathcal{P}}

The Kleisli category of the Giry monad has the same objects as Meas. A morphism from X to Y is a measurable function k: X\to\mathcal{P}(Y), i.e., a Markov kernel. The identity morphism is \eta_X. Kleisli composition of k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) and \ell:Y\to\mathcal{P}(Z) is

(\ell \circ_K k)(x)(A) = \int_Y \ell(y)(A)\,k(x,dy), \quad A\in\Sigma_Z.

This is a well‑defined Markov kernel, and composition is associative.

B.3 Cognitive Modules as Kleisli Morphisms

In CFC, each cognitive module is modeled as a Markov kernel k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) where X is an input space (e.g., sensory data, belief parameters) and Y an output space (e.g., actions, updated beliefs). The module’s internal generative model and active inference dynamics determine the kernel.

Definition B.2 (Free energy of a kernel). Fix a “prior” or target probability measure p_Y\in\mathcal{P}(Y). For a kernel k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y), define the pointwise free energy by

\mathcal{F}_X[k](x) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(k(x,\cdot)\,\|\,p_Y\big) = \int_Y \ln\!\left(\frac{k(x,dy)}{p_Y(dy)}\right) k(x,dy),

with the convention that the KL divergence is +\infty if k(x,\cdot) is not absolutely continuous w.r.t. p_Y.

B.4 Free‑Energy Bound Under Composition

Theorem B.3 (Free energy preservation). Let k:X\to\mathcal{P}(Y) and \ell:Y\to\mathcal{P}(Z) be Markov kernels, and let target measures p_Y\in\mathcal{P}(Y), p_Z\in\mathcal{P}(Z) be given. Assume all measures are absolutely continuous where needed. Then for every x\in X,

\mathcal{F}_X[\ell\circ_K k](x) \le \mathcal{F}_X[k](x) + \int_Y \mathcal{F}_Y[\ell](y)\,k(x,dy).
\tag{B.1}

Proof. Let q(x,dy)=k(x,dy) and r(y,dz)=\ell(y,dz). The composition yields the measure on Z:

(r\circ q)(x,dz) = \int_Y r(y,dz)\,q(x,dy).

The KL divergence from this measure to p_Z is

\begin{aligned}
&\; D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(r\circ q\,\|\,p_Z\big) \\
&= \int_Z \ln\!\left(\frac{\int_Y r(y,dz)q(x,dy)}{p_Z(dz)}\right) \int_Y r(y,dz)q(x,dy) \\
&= \int_Y \left[ \int_Z \ln\!\left(\frac{\int_{Y} r(\tilde y,dz)q(x,d\tilde y)}{p_Z(dz)}\right) r(y,dz) \right] q(x,dy).
\end{aligned}

Now apply the conditional Jensen’s inequality to the inner integral. The function \phi(t)=t\ln t is convex on (0,\infty). For fixed y, the inner measure is an average over r(y,\cdot). Thus,

\begin{aligned}
\int_Z \ln\!\left(\frac{\int r(\tilde y,dz)q(d\tilde y)}{p_Z(dz)}\right) r(y,dz)
&\le \int_Z \ln\!\left(\frac{r(y,dz)}{p_Z(dz)}\right) r(y,dz) \\
&= D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(r(y,\cdot)\,\|\,p_Z\big) = \mathcal{F}_Y[\ell](y).
\end{aligned}

Adding the term coming from the outer integral (which originally contains the average inside the log), a more careful application uses the chain rule for KL divergence:

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\otimes r\,\|\,p_Y\otimes p_Z) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\,\|\,p_Y) + \int_Y D_{\mathrm{KL}}(r(y,\cdot)\,\|\,p_Z)\,q(dy),

where q\otimes r is the joint distribution on Y\times Z. Since marginalizing over Y can only decrease KL (data processing inequality), we have

D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big((r\circ q)\,\|\,p_Z\big) \le D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\otimes r\,\|\,p_Y\otimes p_Z) = \mathcal{F}_X[k](x) + \int_Y \mathcal{F}_Y[\ell](y)\,k(x,dy),

which is exactly the claimed inequality. ∎

This result guarantees that composing inference modules does not lead to an explosion of free energy, thereby enforcing the thermodynamic stability of the cognitive field at the architectural level.

