Appendix C: Operator‑Splitting Numerical Scheme and Convergence

We present a fully discrete numerical method for the coupled fractional reaction–diffusion / mean‑field game system and prove its convergence under a CFL condition adapted to the fractional derivative.

C.1 Time Discretisation

Let \Delta t = T/N_t be the time step, t_n = n\Delta t. We split the evolution into three substeps:

1. Fractional diffusion (knowledge spread).
2. BUDT reaction (local inference).
3. Mean‑field game update (agent movement and value function).

C.2 Step 1: Fractional Diffusion

Discretize the homogeneous fractional PDE

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi = \nabla\cdot(D\nabla\Psi)

using a Grünwald–Letnikov approximation of the Caputo derivative. For a function u^n at grid points, the m-th order approximation is

{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha u^n \approx \frac{1}{(\Delta t)^\alpha} \sum_{j=0}^{n} c_j^{(\alpha)} u^{n-j},

where c_0^{(\alpha)}=1 and c_j^{(\alpha)} = \left(1-\frac{1+\alpha}{j}\right) c_{j-1}^{(\alpha)}. This yields an implicit scheme:

\frac{1}{(\Delta t)^\alpha} \sum_{j=0}^{n+1} c_j^{(\alpha)} \Psi^{n+1-j} = \nabla\cdot(D(\Psi^n)\nabla\Psi^{n+1}).
\tag{C.1}

With Neumann boundaries, the elliptic operator is invertible. The truncation error is O((\Delta t)^{2-\alpha}) for sufficiently smooth solutions (Lin & Xu, 2007).

C.3 Step 2: BUDT Reaction

Given \Psi^{(1)} from the diffusion step, solve the local maximization:

\Psi^{(2)}(\mathbf{x}_i) = \Psi^{(1)}(\mathbf{x}_i) + \Delta t \cdot F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi^{(1)}(\mathbf{x}_i)),

where F_{\text{BUDT}} is the optimal action from (9.1). Since this term involves no spatial coupling, it is fully parallelizable.

C.4 Step 3: Mean‑Field Game Update

The MFG system (7.5)–(7.6) is solved via a finite difference scheme. For brevity we give the scheme for the simplified case with only spatial variables. The HJB equation is discretized backward in time:

\frac{u_i^{n} - u_i^{n+1}}{\Delta t} = \nu \Delta_h u_i^{n+1} - \frac12 |\nabla_h u_i^{n+1}|^2 + \mathcal{F}(\Psi_i^{n+1},m_i^{n+1}),

where \Delta_h and \nabla_h are discrete Laplacian and gradient. The Fokker–Planck equation is advanced forward:

\frac{m_i^{n+1} - m_i^{n}}{\Delta t} = \nu \Delta_h m_i^{n+1} + \nabla_h\cdot\big(m_i^{n}\nabla_h u_i^{n+1}\big).

A Picard iteration within each time step ensures consistency between u and m. The scheme is conservative and preserves positivity of m.

C.5 Convergence Theorem

Theorem C.1 (Convergence of splitting scheme). Assume the exact solution (\Psi,m,u) is sufficiently smooth. Let the numerical solution be \Psi_h^n, m_h^n, u_h^n with spatial mesh size h and time step \Delta t. Under the CFL condition \Delta t \le C h^{2} for the integer‑order part and \Delta t \le C h^{2/\alpha} for the fractional part, there exists a constant C_T such that

\|\Psi_h^n - \Psi(t_n)\|_{L^2} + \|m_h^n - m(t_n)\|_{L^1} + \|u_h^n - u(t_n)\|_{H^1} \le C_T\big(h^2 + (\Delta t)^{\min(2-\alpha,1)}\big).

Proof sketch. The splitting introduces a first‑order error in time (unless symmetrised). Consistency of the fractional derivative discretization gives an error O((\Delta t)^{2-\alpha}). Stability of the implicit diffusion step and the MFG solver is ensured under the CFL conditions. A standard error analysis using the Lax equivalence theorem for each substep, combined with the Lipschitz properties of the reaction and the MFG coupling, yields the overall error bound. ∎

C.6 Memory Truncation

The Grünwald–Letnikov sum extends to t=0. For long simulations, the full history becomes prohibitive. However, because |c_j^{(\alpha)}|\sim \Gamma(1-\alpha)^{-1} j^{-1-\alpha}, the summands decay polynomially. One can truncate the history after N_{\tau} steps such that the tail error is below \epsilon, with N_{\tau} \sim (\epsilon \Gamma(1-\alpha))^{-1/\alpha}. This provides a practical fixed‑memory implementation.

